WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:09.735
This is the Korea Daily Brief from the CSIS Korea Chair, read by an AI voice. It's Saturday, November 14.

2
00:00:09.735 --> 00:00:26.921
Three things today: experts warn Trump may use wartime operational control transfer to pull troops from Korea, China briefs Seoul on Kim's secret Dandong visit, and North Korea's naval buildup accelerates as Kim inspects a second east-coast base.

3
00:00:26.921 --> 00:00:37.610
The troop withdrawal warning lands just as China is quietly shaping the terms of any future U.S.-North Korea diplomacy, and Seoul is caught between both.

4
00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:52.132
First, the wartime operational control alarm. Experts are warning that the Trump administration may use the transfer of wartime operational control to South Korea as cover for withdrawing U.S. troops from the peninsula.

5
00:00:52.132 --> 00:00:56.998
That's the signal embedded in today's re-line, drawing on reporting from Yonhap.

6
00:00:56.998 --> 00:01:07.522
For Korea policymakers, the concern is structural: a transfer that is meant to strengthen the alliance could instead be used to justify reducing the U.S. military footprint.

7
00:01:07.522 --> 00:01:16.951
The brief flags this as a live development, not a hypothetical. Watch how Seoul responds in the days before President Lee attends the Shenzhen APEC summit.

8
00:01:16.951 --> 00:01:28.144
Any signal from Washington on wartime operational control will land in a charged diplomatic environment, with a planned Lee-Xi bilateral on the sidelines and North Korea on the agenda.

9
00:01:28.144 --> 00:01:38.120
The bottom line here: the wartime operational control transfer, long a technical alliance management question, may now be carrying a much heavier political freight.

10
00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:42.867
Second, what China told Seoul about Kim's Dandong visit.

11
00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:54.268
Chinese Foreign Minister Wang Yi gave South Korean Foreign Minister Cho Hyun a detailed account of Kim Jong Un's October 24 visit to Dandong during talks in Beijing on November 13.

12
00:01:54.268 --> 00:02:01.869
The two met for approximately three hours, including dinner. It was the first ministerial meeting in about three months.

13
00:02:01.869 --> 00:02:10.547
A South Korean official said China described its relationship with North Korea as focused on economic cooperation, not political affairs.

14
00:02:10.547 --> 00:02:21.758
That framing matters: it is Beijing's way of signaling it is not coordinating with Pyongyang on security or nuclear issues, even as Kim was moving through a Chinese border city.

15
00:02:21.758 --> 00:02:30.879
The same official assessed a U.S.-North Korea summit as possible but not highly likely, saying it would depend on President Trump's willingness.

16
00:02:30.879 --> 00:02:44.751
Foreign Minister Cho called on China to play a constructive role in bringing North Korea back to dialogue. Wang reaffirmed Beijing's unchanged Korea Peninsula policy and welcomed President Lee's planned APEC attendance.

17
00:02:44.751 --> 00:02:49.121
After the meeting, Cho said South Korea should not fear China's rise.

18
00:02:49.121 --> 00:02:58.686
He posted on social media that he would work until the last moment to make Lee's APEC visit an important occasion for developing Korea-China relations.

19
00:02:58.686 --> 00:03:08.250
The key fact: China told Seoul the Kim-Dandong visit was about economics, not politics. Seoul is not yet persuaded a U.S.-North Korea summit is coming.

20
00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:11.640
Third, North Korea's naval buildup.

21
00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:24.154
Kim Jong Un inspected a second major east-coast naval base under construction on November 12 and 13, ordering that it be completed on the schedule and to the quality standard set by the party central.

22
00:03:24.154 --> 00:03:29.598
This follows a separate inspection of a first east-coast base, also reported this week.

23
00:03:29.598 --> 00:03:50.372
In a quote carried by state media on November 13, Kim said, "Modern naval base construction is a major core project directly linked to our state's safety and defense of maritime sovereignty, and must unconditionally be completed within the period decided by the party central and at the quality level demanded by the party central."

24
00:03:50.372 --> 00:03:59.069
Separately, N.K. News reports that North Korea's announced November 4 cluster warhead test was fabricated using doctored imagery from 2022.

25
00:03:59.069 --> 00:04:05.827
That finding adds to what state media describe as a pattern of domestic weapons-test blackouts. And the U.S.

26
00:04:05.827 --> 00:04:17.966
Department of Justice moved to freeze nearly 1.2 billion dollars in funds tied to North Korea's hack of the Binance crypto exchange, the largest single crypto-seizure action targeting Pyongyang.

27
00:04:17.966 --> 00:04:21.408
The hack involved more than 6 billion dollars in total.

28
00:04:21.408 --> 00:04:32.170
The picture across these three items is of a regime building hard military capacity, faking tests for a domestic audience, and continuing to fund it all through cybercrime.

29
00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:46.832
Now to Pyongyang. State media led today with one article flagged in today's brief: N.K. News reporting that North Korea's November 4 announced cluster warhead test was fabricated using doctored imagery from 2022.

30
00:04:46.832 --> 00:04:57.119
State media itself did not acknowledge the fabrication. Separately, KCNA carried Kim's inspection of the second east-coast naval base, which a lead story covered.

31
00:04:57.119 --> 00:05:03.532
That report is state media's way of projecting urgency and party discipline around the naval buildup.

32
00:05:03.532 --> 00:05:12.740
The bottom line: Pyongyang is managing its weapons narrative carefully, suppressing what it cannot control and amplifying what projects strength.

33
00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:24.486
The rest of the news, quickly. The Wall Street Journal reports Kim Jong Un has formally modified North Korea's national flag, breaking from the Soviet-era design.

34
00:05:24.486 --> 00:05:34.122
The change was formalized alongside broader constitutional revisions that expanded Kim's powers and codified a two-state framework with South Korea.

35
00:05:34.122 --> 00:05:44.212
Russia and North Korea are set to run 14 daily bus round trips over the new Tumangang bridge. Experts say workers, not tourists, are the likely passengers.

36
00:05:44.212 --> 00:05:54.629
Six North Korean workers were killed in a train crash in Russia's Far East in October, according to Daily N.K.. No accountability mechanism was identified. U.S.

37
00:05:54.629 --> 00:06:07.258
media reported a possible Trump-Xi-Putin trilateral meeting at the Shenzhen APEC summit on November 19, with North Korea cited as a possible agenda item. No official schedule has been confirmed.

38
00:06:07.258 --> 00:06:17.349
Nuclear arms control was cited as the primary U.S. goal. Iran has warned South Korea over an expanded South Korean naval mission, according to Dong-A Ilbo.

39
00:06:17.349 --> 00:06:30.630
South Korea held a separate memorial ceremony for Korean victims of the Sado mine, maintaining pressure on Japan to fully acknowledge the history of wartime forced labor at the UNESCO World Heritage site.

40
00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:43.780
South Korea is seeking European Union exemptions for Korean steel exports and nuclear equipment. South Korea's national football team ended a 21-year unbeaten streak against Saudi Arabia, losing 3 to 2.

41
00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:46.515
The last loss to Saudi Arabia was in 2005.

42
00:06:46.515 --> 00:06:58.363
South Korea's information and communications technology ministry is forming a joint emergency team with Microsoft Korea after a Microsoft services outage caused domestic disruptions.

43
00:06:58.363 --> 00:07:08.259
The National Assembly passed what is being called the fabricated prosecution special counsel bill on November 14, driven by the ruling Democratic Party.

44
00:07:08.259 --> 00:07:20.042
People Power Party leader Jang Dong-hyuk called the bill 100 percent unconstitutional and demanded a presidential veto. The vote triggered a major People Power Party rally in Busan.

45
00:07:20.042 --> 00:07:33.844
A Gallup Korea poll taken November 3 to 5 put presidential approval at 41 percent, up from the prior reading. The Democratic Party stood at 36 percent, also up. The People Power Party came in at 28 percent, down.

46
00:07:33.844 --> 00:07:44.390
Independents were also at 28 percent, stable. CSIS announced that William Daley has been named Chair of the CSIS Board of Trustees and Paul Ryan named Vice Chair.

47
00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:55.163
A CSIS podcast episode, the 79th in the Capital Cable series, examines the roles China and Russia play in enabling North Korea's human rights abuses.

48
00:07:55.163 --> 00:08:04.790
On November 17, the Hudson Institute hosts a panel on overcoming U.S.-South Korean alliance challenges covering trade, technology, and China.

49
00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:17.107
On Capitol Hill. The U.S. House passed a bill to shield small businesses from foreign threats including North Korea, addressing cybersecurity and economic security vulnerabilities.

50
00:08:17.107 --> 00:08:28.223
Separately, congressional momentum is growing for tighter controls on advanced chip exports to China, with bipartisan interest in restricting flows of Nvidia's most advanced chips.

51
00:08:28.223 --> 00:08:34.770
The Korea Times notes that South Korean chipmakers could face secondary effects on their China operations.

52
00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:43.881
Beyond the region. Korea Hydro and Nuclear Power reviewed progress on the Czech nuclear project with its local client.

53
00:08:43.881 --> 00:08:56.417
Industry Minister Kim Jeong-gwan separately assured his Czech counterpart the project is on schedule, and agreed to activate a regular ministerial-level consultative body before year-end.

54
00:08:56.417 --> 00:09:10.830
Kia plans to invest 2 billion dollars in a second manufacturing plant in India. And Malaysia's defense minister confirmed all 18 FA-50M light combat aircraft ordered from South Korea will be delivered by March 2028.

55
00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:23.642
Finally, the markets. Seoul's main stock index, the KOSPI, closed at about 5,291, down roughly 1 percent. The dollar bought about 1,336 won, up slightly.

56
00:09:23.642 --> 00:09:38.670
The Bank of Korea's policy rate stands at 3 percent, last changed in October. That's the Korea Daily Brief for Saturday, November 14. The full text, with a link to every source, is in today's email.
